\section*{1. Project Directory Architecture}
A large-scale thesis requires strict modularity. The recommended directory layout is:

\begin{verbatim}
my_thesis/
├── main.tex
├── preamble.sty
├── bibliography.bib
├── chapters/
│   ├── 01_introduction.tex
│   ├── 02_literature.tex
│   ├── 03_methodology.tex
│   └── ...
├── frontmatter/
│   ├── titlepage.tex
│   ├── abstract.tex
│   ├── acknowledgments.tex
│   └── dedication.tex
└── figures/
\end{verbatim}

This structure isolates content, improves editor responsiveness, and ensures clean compilation.

\section*{2. Document Class Selection}
For manuscripts exceeding 200 pages, use:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
    \item \textbf{memoir} — highly recommended; integrates book-level formatting tools.
    \item \textbf{report} — standard multi-chapter class.
\end{itemize}

\section*{3. Master File Template}
\begin{verbatim}
\documentclass[11pt,twoside,openright]{memoir}
\usepackage{preamble}

\begin{document}

\frontmatter
\include{frontmatter/titlepage}
\include{frontmatter/abstract}
\include{frontmatter/acknowledgments}

\tableofcontents
\listoffigures
\listoftables

\mainmatter
\include{chapters/01_introduction}
\include{chapters/02_literature}
\include{chapters/03_methodology}
% Additional chapters...

\backmatter
\include{chapters/appendix}
\printbibliography

\end{document}
\end{verbatim}

\section*{4. Modular Compilation: \texttt{\textbackslash include} vs. \texttt{\textbackslash input}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
    \item \texttt{\include\{...\}} — chapter-level inclusion; supports \texttt{\includeonly} for selective compilation.
    \item \texttt{\input\{...\}} — inline inclusion; ideal for subsections, tables, or TikZ figures.
\end{itemize}

\section*{5. Performance Optimization}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2cm]
    \item \textbf{latexmk} — automates multi-pass compilation.
    \item \textbf{tikzexternalize} — caches TikZ figures to reduce compile time.
    \item \textbf{draft mode} — disables image rendering for rapid iteration.
\end{itemize}

\section*{Summary}
A 300-page thesis must be treated as a software project: modular, maintainable, and optimized. The architecture above ensures stable compilation, clean structure, and professional output suitable for scientific publication.

